.TH KOSH 1 "2026-08-18" "kosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kosh \- a fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell,
formatter, and language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kosh
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-s
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-c
.IR command ...]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-\-format ]
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kosh
is the Koshka shell executable. It is an interpreter, an interactive shell, a
formatter, a language server, and a diagnostic tool. It accepts full Bash and
POSIX shell syntax with Bash-compatible extensions.
.PP
Koshka includes about 300 built-in ShellCheck diagnostics. Script execution is
about five times faster than Bash, and analysis is about 100 times faster than
ShellCheck.
.PP
Koshka supports Linux, macOS, and Windows with equivalent behavior on all
three.
.PP
The grammar is the same on every operating system. An unquoted backslash is an
escape on Windows. A native Windows path uses a quoted backslash, an escaped
backslash, or a forward slash. After quote removal, Windows path
operations accept both slash forms and recognize drive and UNC roots. POSIX
path operations accept only a forward slash.
.SH INVOCATION
The input source is selected in this order. The
.B \-s
option selects standard input. One or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings in occurrence order. A script operand
selects that file. The
.B \-i
option or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor when no
earlier source applies. Standard input is used otherwise.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-lint
analyzes each command string in occurrence order, followed by every file
operand. Lint operation is never interactive, and the shell exits after the
last selected input.
Under
.BR \-s ,
standard input is analyzed and a file operand becomes a positional parameter.
With no command string and no file operand, standard input is analyzed.
.PP
The
.B \-\-format
option reads standard input when no file is named. One named file is formatted
to standard output. The
.B \-\-apply
option writes the result to one or more named files. Under
.BR \-\-lint ,
safe diagnostic fixes are written. When
.B \-\-lint
and
.B \-\-format
are combined, the lint fixes are written first and the result is then
formatted.
.B \-\-lint \-\-format
without
.B \-\-apply
writes the fixed and formatted source to standard output.
.PP
The
.B \-\-format
option separates a declaration block from the following command. It also
separates a completed fi, done, esac, subshell, or brace group from the next
independent command. Lines are kept within 78 columns. A word that cannot be
broken to fit is left whole, and a warning names its file, line, and column.
.PP
When both
.B \-s
and
.B \-i
are present outside lint operation, a terminal on standard input selects
interactive operation. A non-terminal standard input is read as input. The
.B \-s
option takes precedence over command strings and file operands. Command strings
take precedence over
.BR \-i .
A script operand takes precedence over
.BR \-i .
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood. The names
.B sh
and
.B dash
select the
.B sh
mood. The names
.B bash
and
.B rbash
select the
.B bash
mood. The name
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero or the
.B \-l
option selects login operation.
.PP
The
.B KOSH_FLAGS
environment variable supplies options before the command line is parsed. A
command-line option overrides the corresponding value from
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
The
.B \-c
option is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
The
.BR \-\-format ,
.BR \-\-apply ,
and
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
options are also ignored there.
.PP
The shell enters rescue mode when argument zero begins with a dash, its options
cannot be parsed, and standard input is a terminal. Rescue mode clears the
options and skips startup files. The
.B \-l
option alone does not enable this recovery. Any other invalid invocation exits
with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version and notices are displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
The short version is displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Help is displayed. The shell then exits.
.SS POSIX options
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
The shell runs interactively.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Commands are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fICOMMAND\fR ", " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
COMMAND is read and run. The option may be repeated, and the command strings
run in the order they are given.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
The shell exits after an unguarded failing command. A function called from a
non-final operand of
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
or from a negation, stays guarded through its whole body.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Pathname expansion is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
One command is read and run. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Each input line is printed as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Each command is printed after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Each later assignment is marked for export.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
An existing file is not overwritten by a plain output redirection.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Commands are parsed and analyzed without being run. The analysis stage runs in
every mood under this option, and a compatibility mood still reports only the
tiers selected by
.BR \-W .
A runtime
.B set \-n
also suppresses later commands in the current command list.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
An unset variable read is an error.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
The shell acts as a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-m
The options are accepted without effect for compatibility.
.TP
.B \-\-
Option parsing ends. The next operand is treated as the script name.
.SS Bash options
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-rcfile " " \fIFILE\fR
FILE selects the interactive rc file. The last occurrence of either name is
used.
A leading tilde is expanded. The file replaces /etc/koshrc and ~/.koshrc in the
.B kosh
mood. The file replaces ~/.bashrc in an interactive non-login Bash mood. The
system Bash rc is still read first.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
The interactive non-login Bash rc and a custom rc file are skipped. Login
profiles and noninteractive BASH_ENV processing are unchanged. The stock Bash
completion script is not loaded.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
The shell becomes restricted after the startup files finish.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Privileged mode is enabled. BASH_ENV is not sourced. When the real and
effective ids differ, every startup file is skipped. Elevated ids are retained
only when this option is present. The state is exposed through
.BR $\- ,
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
and
.BR "set \-o privileged" .
.TP
.B \-\-posix
The
.B bash-posix
mood is selected.
.SS Compatibility options
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
NAME selects
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.BR bash-posix .
The name
.B default
is accepted for kosh, and
.B posix
and
.B dash
are accepted for sh.
When no mood is chosen and the shell runs a script file operand, the first line
of the script selects the mood. A bash shebang selects
.BR bash ,
an sh or dash shebang selects
.BR sh ,
and a kosh shebang selects
.BR kosh .
The interpreter may be named through
.BR env ,
as in
.B "#!/usr/bin/env bash"
or
.BR "#!/usr/bin/env \-S bash \-e" .
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the
.B kosh
mood. The mood is chosen before startup files are read. An explicit
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
or a shell invocation name such as sh or bash takes precedence over the shebang.
The shebang is not read for
.BR \-c ,
standard input, or
.BR \-\-lint .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Startup files for each listed mood are sourced in order. LIST accepts
comma-separated names. The option may be repeated. The selected runtime mood
is used when the option is omitted.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-enable\-mimicry
A script run as a command with a recognized shell shebang runs under the
corresponding mood. A script operand always does, with or without this
option. A recognized shell invocation name, an explicit
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
or
.B \-\-posix
takes precedence over the shebang. The current diagnostic setting is retained.
When
.B set
changes the option, launched shells inherit it through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS .
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
The options
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-\-no\-completion ,
.BR "\-\-tab\-selector plain" ,
and
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
are applied. Color is disabled. An explicit
.B \-\-tab\-selector
overrides the plain selector, wherever it appears.
.SS Auxiliary options
.TP
.B \-\-lint
The option behaves like
.B \-n
with every diagnostic tier enabled at its normal severity. Diagnostic
suppression from
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
and
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
is overridden. Every startup file is skipped. Aliases, functions, and the search
path from the invoking session do not affect the report.
.PP
The kind of a named input is detected from its path, extension, and content.
Shell commands are extracted from GitHub Actions, Gitea Actions, Forgejo
Actions, GitLab CI, Ansible, Dockerfile and Containerfile, Compose, Markdown,
Makefile, Google Cloud Build, CircleCI, Azure Pipelines, Bitbucket Pipelines,
Buildkite, Travis CI, Kubernetes, package.json scripts, Drone, Woodpecker,
Taskfile, Justfile, RPM spec files, VSCode tasks, and Dev Container files. Only
the extracted sh, bash, or kosh source is parsed and diagnosed. Jenkinsfile,
Terraform, Nix, and an unrecognized
YAML, JSON, or Markdown host are not treated as whole shell files.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
safe structured fixes are written to each named file. The updated source is
analyzed again, and only the remaining diagnostics are reported. The written
fixes are then summarized, with one line for each diagnostic slug and its
ShellCheck code, counted as warnings or errors. A warning-only result exits
successfully. A remaining error or an input or write failure exits with status
1.
.TP
.B \-\-format
Shell source is formatted without being run. Structural keywords and compound
braces are placed on separate lines. Each nested level uses two spaces. Direct
.B test
commands become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
the name. Code is wrapped at 80 columns when a safe token boundary exists.
Standalone comments follow the surrounding code indentation and are not
wrapped. Inline comments remain inline. Here-document bodies and terminators
retain their layout, and the shell source around them is still formatted.
Output uses LF line endings and one final newline for a nonempty script.
Formatted source uses syntax colors when standard output is a terminal.
With no file or a lone
.B \-
operand, source is read from standard input. One named file is formatted to
standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Named files are updated with safe lint fixes under
.BR \-\-lint ,
or with formatted source under
.BR \-\-format .
The two modes may be combined. Before a changed file is replaced atomically, its
contents and identity are checked again. File modes and symbolic links are
retained. Other hard links continue to refer to the previous inode. Standard
input,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-c ,
and a lone
.B \-
operand are rejected. This option is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.TP
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
The language server protocol is run over standard input and standard output.
The editor starts this mode and associates it with
.BR *.sh ,
.BR *.dash ,
.BR *.bash ,
and
.BR *.kosh .
The server provides diagnostics, quick fixes, completion, navigation, command
information, semantic tokens, a document outline, and rename. A fix-all action
applies every non-conflicting safe fix in the open document. At command
position, completion includes shell keywords, builtins, bundled utilities,
functions and aliases defined in the document, and commands from the active
PATH.
The client receives keyword items as keywords and all other items as functions.
Each command item contains the completed name. The client requests its help text
when it resolves the item. Help text is fetched only for the highlighted item.
.PP
The server recognizes embedded shell regions in the host formats described
under
.BR \-\-lint .
Language features operate only inside those regions. The editor's host language
service handles the host syntax.
The outline is a tree of
the function definitions and the variable assignments in the document. An
assignment inside a function body is a child of that function, and one name
assigned again in the same body is one outline row. A name bound by
.BR local ,
.BR declare ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR export ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR for ,
.BR select ,
.BR read ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR printf " " \-v ,
or an arithmetic assignment is a variable assignment too. Hover over a variable
reports the nearest preceding assignment and its folded value. If the assignment
has no folded value, hover describes its source, such as a loop word, a field
read from input, or an arithmetic expression. Earlier assignments in the same
document are listed below it. An assignment that does not run on every path is
marked. A shell-defined variable uses the description from the builtin variable
catalog. Hover over a function shows its definition body as printed by
.BR "declare \-f" .
Hover over a command the document does not define reports the external
help text. A builtin is checked first, followed by a program on the active PATH,
then a bundled utility when PATH has no matching program. Markdown is used when
the client advertises support for it.
Rename updates variables, functions, and aliases throughout the open document. A
read written as
.B $name
or
.B ${name}
keeps its sigils and braces. Only the name bytes are replaced. A command can be
renamed only when the document defines it as a function or alias. Builtins,
bundled utilities, and programs on the active PATH cannot be renamed. A new
variable name may contain letters, digits, and underscores, but may not begin
with a digit. A new command name may not contain shell metacharacters. A
recognized shebang selects the mood first. If there is no recognized shebang,
the client language identifier selects it. If the client sends no recognized
identifier, a recognized document extension selects it. An unrecognized document
uses the
.B kosh
mood. This option does not accept file operands,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
or
.BR \-\-apply .
It is ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.SS Koshka options
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-no\-init\-files
No startup file is read. PATH is set to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-WW ", " \-WWW
The analysis stage has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers. In the default
mood, strict and lenient diagnostics are errors, while annoying diagnostics are
warnings. The
.B \-W
form retains those severities,
.B \-WW
demotes lenient errors to warnings, and
.B \-WWW
also demotes strict errors. In other moods,
.B \-W
enables strict diagnostics as warnings,
.B \-WW
also enables lenient diagnostics, and
.B \-WWW
also enables annoying diagnostics. An explicit
.B set \-\-mood
change clears the active level. A level changed with
.B set
is inherited by launched shells through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS ,
and an explicit level on the command line replaces it. In the Bash and POSIX
moods no analysis runs for the script or the files it sources until a level is
set, and an inherited
.BI warnings= N
counts as set. The
.B mimicry
word alone sets no level. Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
The available analysis checks and their metadata are listed. The shell then
exits.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
The analysis stage is skipped, and no warning or pre-run diagnostic is reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
The annoying tier is suppressed. Strict and lenient analysis is unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Diagnostics and warnings are suppressed while startup files are sourced. The
previous settings are restored before interactive input begins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Source backtraces are suppressed. The primary message, source caret, note, and
detail location remain visible when available.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Tab completion and ghost text are disabled.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Syntax coloring and ghost text are disabled. Tab completion remains enabled.
.TP
.BR \-\-tab\-selector " " \fIMODE\fR
MODE selects how an explicit Tab presents several candidates and how Ctrl\-R
presents the history. The names are
.BR interactive ,
.BR external ,
and
.BR plain .
The default is
.BR interactive .
The behavior of each mode is described under
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-koshkit
Bundled utility names resolve directly as commands. The option is equivalent to
.BR "set \-o koshkit" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic
Arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values are enabled. The option
is equivalent to
.BR "set \-o extended\-arithmetic" .
.SS Debug options
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
Syntax trees are printed before execution and during formatting or linting.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics
Analysis prepass decisions are reported. Each folded or eliminated node produces
a lenient analysis diagnostic. The final line reports the number of eliminated
statements. A compatibility mood reports these diagnostics only under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-exit\-code
A located diagnostic is printed when a command returns a non-zero status. A
warning is printed when execution continues. An error is printed before
errexit exits. A pipeline reports its effective status after pipefail is
applied. The
.B "set \-o show\-exit\-code"
form turns the diagnostic on while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
Every command status is reported, including a successful zero. A zero status is
always a warning. A non-zero status keeps the wording and the severity of
.BR \-\-show\-exit\-code .
The
.BR "set \-N" " and " "set +N"
forms turn the diagnostic on and off while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
The escape bitmap is printed after each parsed command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Command, expansion, node, and arena statistics are printed after each command.
The report also counts the builtins, functions, and external commands this
shell ran, the processes it forked and executed, the bytes and system calls it
read and wrote, and its resource usage: user and system time, peak resident
set, context switches, and page faults. A figure the platform cannot report is
omitted. Windows counts each created process as one fork and one exec.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Arena and heap usage is printed at exit.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-debug\-logging =\fILEVEL\fR
Internal logging is enabled at
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
or
.BR all .
An unknown level is a usage error. This option is available only in a debug
build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-logging\-file =\fIFILE\fR
Debug logging is appended to FILE. A missing file is created. Standard error is
used when the option is omitted or the file cannot be opened. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-complete\-at =\fILINE\fR
Completion candidates for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option
is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-highlight\-at =\fILINE\fR
Highlight spans for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-ghost\-at =\fILINE\fR
The ghost completion result and operation counts for LINE are printed. The
shell then exits. This option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-brackets\-at =\fILINE\fR
The bracket pair matched at each caret offset of LINE is printed. The shell
then exits. This option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-arena\-lifetimes
Arena lifetime identities are checked across every release and reset. This
option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-toiletline\-allocation
Allocation failure handling in the line editor is checked. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Function or system-call tracing is enabled. These options are available only in
a Cosmopolitan build.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand names the script file. A lone
.B \-
operand selects standard input. A directory operand fails with status 126. A
file that cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script invocation, the script pathname becomes $0. Arguments after the
script become positional parameters. For a
.B \-c
invocation, the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. Later operands become
positional parameters. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 remains the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-lint
treats every operand as an input file and no operand becomes a positional
parameter. Missing, unreadable, and directory inputs are reported before the
remaining inputs are analyzed.
.SH MOODS
The
.B kosh
mood enables strict checks, pipefail, failglob, nounset, extended arithmetic,
and analysis by default. The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis disabled by default. The
.B sh
mood follows the supported dash behavior. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled and reads the Bash startup
files.
.PP
An explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
.BR "set \-o failglob" ,
or
.B set \-o extended\-arithmetic
survives a later mood change. The command
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix .
The command
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
only from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
On Windows, the default mood leaves backslashes literal in
.BR echo .
The
.B \-e
option enables escape decoding. The
.B sh
mood retains dash-style default escape handling.
.SH STARTUP FILES
Each startup file is parsed with the grammar of the mood whose files are being
sourced. A startup file that invokes
.B "set \-\-init\-moods"
does not reenter a mood whose files are already being sourced.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B kosh
or
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile in that order. An interactive shell in
the
.B kosh
mood then sources /etc/koshrc and ~/.koshrc. A custom rc file replaces that
pair.
.PP
An interactive shell in the
.B sh
mood sources the file named by ENV when privileged mode is disabled. The ENV
value is used as a pathname without PATH lookup.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile. It then sources the first readable file among
~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile.
.PP
An interactive non-login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources the first readable file among /etc/bash/bashrc and
/etc/bash.bashrc. It then sources the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc. The file
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion is sourced after the rc file. It is
skipped when completion is disabled, when a default completion specification
exists, and when BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is already set.
.PP
A noninteractive shell in the
.B bash
mood sources BASH_ENV once. Parameter expansion, command substitution,
arithmetic expansion, and leading-tilde expansion are applied to the value.
PATH is not searched. A shell in the
.B bash-posix
mood does not source BASH_ENV.
.PP
A clean, rescue, or lint shell skips every startup file. A shell whose real and
effective identities differ skips them too. The
privileged option suppresses ENV and BASH_ENV processing. Restricted mode is
visible while startup files run. Its restrictions become active after startup
finishes.
.PP
Settings for pipefail and failglob that come from the mood remain disabled while
startup files run. An explicit command-line nounset setting remains active. The
selected mood's strictness takes effect after startup finishes.
.PP
Missing optional files, custom rc files, and BASH_ENV files are skipped. An
existing custom rc or BASH_ENV file that cannot be read produces a diagnostic
and is skipped. An unreadable ENV file is skipped without that diagnostic. See
.BR kosh (5)
for the startup file format.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes and changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV,
and BASH_ENV. A directory separator is rejected in a command name, a source
operand, a history path, and a
.B hash \-p
path.
.PP
The redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and >& are rejected. Numeric
descriptor duplication remains available. The
.B exec
builtin,
.BR "command \-p" ,
and
.B enable \-f
or
.B enable \-d
are rejected. Restricted mode cannot be disabled with
.BR "set +r" .
Changes to the restricted_shell shopt value are accepted without effect.
.PP
A sourced script retains the restrictions. A shell script found through PATH
and executed as a command runs without them.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described below. The
.B sh
mood disables Bash-only syntax except for documented compatibility forms.
Compatibility differences are listed under
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
Constants may be folded and an unreachable command may be removed before the
script runs. The init clause of a C-style for loop still runs once when the
condition folds to zero.
.PP
An if command with no selected branch and no else command returns status zero.
This rule also applies at the end of a sourced file.
.SS Coprocesses
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
The
.B bash
mood accepts a coprocess. The command runs asynchronously and two pipes connect
it to the shell. NAME[0] is read from, NAME[1] is written to, and NAME_PID holds
the process id. The default name is COPROC.
.PP
A word before the command names the coprocess only when a compound command
follows that word. In
.B coproc NAME cat
the word NAME is the first word of a simple command and the coprocess takes the
default name. The launch itself returns status zero.
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve every enclosed byte. Double quotes retain parameter,
command, and arithmetic expansion. A backslash quotes the next byte where the
grammar permits it.
.PP
The form $'...' reads C escapes including \en, \et, \exHH, and \eOOO. The form
$"..." marks a string for translation. Every mood accepts it, and it behaves as
an ordinary double-quoted string when no catalog is present. It is recognized
only outside double quotes. Within a double-quoted string, $" is a literal
dollar followed by the closing quote. Under an sh or dash shebang, analysis
reports it as a bash extension absent from POSIX sh.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional parameters
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. The form "$@" preserves one
field per positional parameter. The form "$*" joins the parameters with the
first byte of IFS.
.SS Indexed arrays
An indexed array is assigned with a=(x y z). An element is assigned with
a[2]=z. The form a+=(w) appends elements. ${a[i]} reads an arithmetic subscript.
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list elements. ${#a[@]} counts elements. ${!a[@]} lists
indices. A negative index counts from the end. Sparse indices remain absent
from ${!a[@]}.
.PP
Inside double quotes, "${a[@]}" preserves one field per element.
"${a[*]}" joins elements with the first byte of IFS. An unset array read through
${a[@]} is an error under
.BR "set \-u" .
.SS Associative arrays
The command
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array. The form m[key]=v assigns an element. The form
${m[key]} reads it. The form ${!m[@]} lists keys. The command
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one element.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.SS Parameter expansion
The form ${v:offset:length} selects a substring. A negative offset requires a
leading space, as in ${v: \-3}. Without the space, ${v:\-3} is the default-value
form. A negative length excludes that many characters from the end.
.PP
The form ${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match. The form ${v//pat/rep} replaces
every match. The anchors /# and /% select the start and end. The
.B patsub_replacement
shopt option is enabled by default. While it is enabled, an unquoted ampersand
in the replacement denotes the matched text, and so does an ampersand that comes
from an unquoted expansion. A quoted ampersand and the form \e& denote a literal
ampersand. When the option is disabled, every ampersand is literal.
.PP
The forms ${v^^}, ${v,,}, ${v^}, and ${v,} change case with an optional pattern.
The transforms ${v@Q}, ${v@E}, ${v@A}, ${v@P}, and ${v@a} produce a quoted
value, expanded escapes, an assignment, a prompt expansion, or attributes. The
forms ${v@U}, ${v@L}, and ${v@u} apply the corresponding case conversion.
.PP
The form ${!ref} reads the variable named by ref. The forms ${!pre*} and
${!pre@} list names beginning with pre.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
Arithmetic accepts the C operators, ++, \-\-, **, the conditional operator, the
comma operator, and assignment operators. Literals may use 0x hexadecimal, 0
octal, 0b binary, or a base prefix such as 16#ff. A variable value is
re-evaluated as an arithmetic expression. Nested subscripts such as a[b[c]] are
resolved from the inner expression outward. An empty (( )) command returns one.
An empty $(( )) expansion produces zero.
.PP
The
.B extended\-arithmetic
option uses arbitrary-precision integers and accepts finite base-10 decimal
literals. It is enabled by default only in the default mood. When the option is
disabled, signed 64-bit arithmetic uses wrapping operations. Addition and
subtraction use the larger operand scale. Multiplication adds the operand
scales. Division and remainder use the larger operand scale and truncate toward
zero.
.PP
A standalone expression with decimal integer literals on both sides of ^ may
report that ^ is bitwise XOR and suggest ** for exponentiation. Expressions
that contain names, assignments, or another bitwise operator do not produce
this suggestion.
.SS Command substitution
The forms $(command) and `command` replace a command with its output. A command
substitution restores runtime state, shell options, directory stack, working
directory state, and file creation mask when it finishes. A syntax error inside
a command, process, or function substitution is reported at its place in the
outer source, and a script or command string containing one does not run. Every
such error in the source is reported, not only the first. The
same check covers substitutions in double quotes, here-document bodies, and the
words of a parameter expansion or arithmetic expansion, and the subscript of
an array assignment. A substitution nested
more than 64 levels deep is reported as a syntax error, as it is at run time.
.PP
Analysis diagnostics apply to the commands inside command, process, and
function substitutions, and to substitutions in parameter expansion words,
arithmetic expansions, here-document bodies, arithmetic commands, and C-style
for headers. The inner findings use the file's own line and column. The body of a
command, backtick, or process substitution is analyzed as a subshell, so a
variable it assigns is not visible after it, and a read inside it counts as a
read of the parent variable. A function substitution shares the current shell.
A backtick body whose escapes change its text is not analyzed.
.SS Process substitution
The forms <(command) and >(command) supply a descriptor path. The command runs
in a separate process.
.SS Brace expansion
The form {a,b,c} produces alternatives. The forms {1..10}, {a..z}, and
{1..10..2} produce ranges. A numeric range such as {001..10} retains its width.
Braces nest. A lone {a} remains literal.
.SS Field splitting
IFS controls field splitting. A run of an IFS whitespace byte collapses. A run
of a non-whitespace IFS byte produces an empty field where applicable.
.SS Pathname expansion
The
.B extglob
option enables ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !(). The
.B globstar
option enables **. The
.BR dotglob ,
.BR nullglob ,
and
.B failglob
options control leading dots, nonmatches, and failed matches. In the default
mood, a nonmatching glob is fatal. In a compatibility mood with failglob
disabled, the nonmatching pattern remains literal. The warning policy may
report that literal fallback. A glob in command position is fatal in the
default mood and reports a warning in a compatibility mood unless diagnostics
are disabled.
.PP
A nonempty
.B GLOBIGNORE
holds colon\-separated patterns. A pathname expansion result that matches one
pattern is removed, and a glob whose results are all removed follows the
nonmatching rules. A pattern is matched against the whole result. A star in a
pattern crosses a slash only at the end of the pattern. The names . and .. are
never results while GLOBIGNORE is nonempty. A nonempty assignment enables the
.B dotglob
option, an empty assignment leaves it unchanged, and an unset of GLOBIGNORE
disables it. A value imported from the environment filters nothing, and
.B dotglob
is left alone until the shell assigns GLOBIGNORE. The
.B extglob
and
.B nocaseglob
options apply to the patterns.
.PP
The character locale decides how much a ? matches. The locale is selected by
the first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG. These variables are
read from the shell at the time of each match, and an assignment, an unset, an
export, and a prefix assignment all take effect. A value whose codeset after
the dot is UTF\-8 or utf8, in any letter case, selects UTF\-8 when the platform
can select that locale. Any other value selects bytes, and so do a UTF\-8 name
that the platform cannot select, an empty value, and no value. This is the way
bash falls back to the C locale.
.PP
In UTF\-8, a ? and each step of a * consume one whole valid UTF\-8 sequence,
and an invalid byte counts as one character. In bytes, each of them consumes
one byte. In UTF\-8, ${#v} and ${v:offset:length} count characters, ${v^},
${v,}, and ${v~} change the case of multibyte letters, and a ${v/pat/rep} whose
pattern holds an invalid UTF\-8 sequence matches by byte. The rule applies to
pathname expansion, case, [[ == ]], ${v#pat}, ${v%pat}, ${v/pat/rep},
${v^pat}, and compgen \-X.
.PP
In UTF\-8, a bracket expression consumes one whole character, its members and
range endpoints are whole characters, a range compares code points, and a
negated bracket still consumes one character. A POSIX class such as [:alpha:]
tests a non\-ASCII character against the Unicode class of the platform, and it
matches nothing on a platform that has no such class. In bytes, a bracket
compares one byte. In UTF\-8, an extglob group splits the subject only at
character boundaries, and its inner patterns follow the same rule. Completion
always matches by UTF\-8 sequence.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
The operators &> and &>> redirect standard output and standard error to one
file. The operator |& pipes both streams. The operator <<< supplies a
here-string followed by one newline.
.PP
A redirection of the form {var}> or {var}< allocates a descriptor numbered ten
or greater and assigns its number to var. The form {var}>&\- closes the
descriptor named by var.
.PP
An appending redirection written inside a loop body opens its file once and
keeps that descriptor for the rest of the loop. The same file and descriptor
number are reused on every iteration, and the descriptor is closed when the loop
ends. Up to sixteen such descriptors are kept at a time, and a loop that appends
to more files than that reopens the ones beyond the limit on every iteration. A
descriptor is held across the iterations whether the command stands alone or
runs as a pipeline stage. Writes therefore continue to the original file even
after that file is renamed or removed. Bash reopens the file on every iteration.
.PP
A diagnostic inside a tab-stripped here-document carries no source caret.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Command lookup checks functions, builtins, cached program paths, PATH, and
bundled koshkit commands when the active mood and options enable them. Running a
command or using the
.B hash
builtin caches its resolved program path. A change to the contents of any PATH
directory clears every cached path.
.B hash \-r
forgets cached command locations.
.B hash \-R
forgets them and rebuilds the PATH command index at once.
.PP
A single directory path in command position changes the
working directory as if it were an operand to
.BR cd .
Functions, builtins, programs, and bundled utilities take precedence. The
.B autocd
shopt option controls this behavior and is enabled by default in every mood.
When the command is made only of dots, two dots move to the parent directory
and each additional dot moves one more parent directory. This shorthand also
requires a single command word, and functions, builtins, programs, and bundled
utilities keep precedence over it.
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when the
typed target is not a directory. Components are resolved in order. An
unavailable path is reported through its first unavailable component. The caret
covers that component.
.PP
A script without a recognized executable format is run by the shell itself. It
receives the command environment and the command argument zero. The caller's
variables, functions, and traps are not inherited. Each complete top-level
command runs before the next one is parsed.
.PP
PIPESTATUS is updated after every foreground command. It contains the status of
each pipeline stage. When
.B pipefail
is enabled, a pipeline returns the status of its rightmost failing stage.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function has its own positional parameters and local variables. Call stack
state is exposed through FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation inherits that
condition. The
.B errexit
option remains suppressed throughout the guarded function body.
.PP
In the default mood the RETURN trap fires at the boundary of every function
body. The
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods fire it for a body that the
.B functrace
option follows and for a body that installs its own action. A sourced file
fires the trap in all of these moods. The
.B sh
mood rejects the RETURN condition.
.SH BUILTINS
The shell provides the POSIX and Bash builtins listed below. The standard forms
follow the active compatibility mood unless a difference is stated here.
.PP
With no operand,
.B builtin
groups the names under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings. The Koshka section
contains assimilate, bench, koshkit, and z. The headings are colored when
standard output is a color terminal.
.PP
.BR : ,
.BR . ,
.BR [ ,
.BR alias ,
.BR bg ,
.BR break ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR compgen ,
.BR compopt ,
.BR continue ,
.BR declare ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR disown ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fc ,
.BR fg ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR let ,
.BR local ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR popd ,
.BR printf ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR return ,
.BR set ,
.BR shift ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR source ,
.BR test ,
.BR time ,
.BR times ,
.BR trap ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR ulimit ,
.BR umask ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR unset ,
and
.BR wait .
.SS Shell-specific builtins
.TP
.BI "assimilate " TARGET
The running executable is copied through scp to TARGET and installed as
.B kosh
in the first usable remote PATH directory. A path containing sbin is skipped.
/usr/local/bin and $HOME/.local/bin are preferred when they occur in PATH. The
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
option requests a relative bash, dash, sh, or kosh link. It may be repeated and
accepts comma-separated values. A candidate directory is skipped when a
requested link path already exists.
.PP
The
.BR \-x " or " \-\-trace
option prints remote progress. The
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
options replace ssh and scp. COMMAND is split at literal spaces.
.PP
The uploaded candidate is validated before the target is replaced. A failure
handled by the transaction restores the previous file or symlink and removes
transaction files. Transactions run one at a time. The next transaction
recovers an interrupted transaction. A failure before the remote transaction
starts leaves the installed target unchanged. An unusable partial upload may
remain.
.TP
.B bench
Commands are sampled through
.BR "kosh \-c" .
The wall time, peak resident set, and available performance counters are
reported as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Later commands are compared
with the first. Sampling stops after a nonzero status unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is present. The
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-\-no\-shell ,
.BR \-\-runs ,
and
.B \-\-duration
options control output, direct execution, sample count, and the millisecond
budget.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-extended ,
each command also reports user and system time, voluntary and involuntary
context switches, minor and major page faults, block input and output
operations, read and write system calls, and bytes read and written. The
times, switches, faults, and block counts cover the sampled process and the
children it waited for. The call and byte counts cover the sampled process
alone, so
.B \-\-no\-shell
measures the command itself. Read and write counts are available on Linux.
Windows reports a single page fault total, and it has no switch or block
counts. A row the platform cannot report is omitted, never printed as zero.
Without the option no extra rows or system calls are added.
.TP
.B z
The current directory is changed to one selected from the frecency store.
Operands form one case-insensitive substring query. Visit count and recency
determine the ranking. With no operand, the home directory is selected.
.SS Compatibility forms
.TP
.B dirs
With no operand, the builtin prints the current directory followed by the saved
stack. A +N operand prints the Nth entry from the top, and a \-N operand prints
the Nth entry from the bottom. The
.B \-v
form keeps the selected entry's full-stack index.
.TP
.B set
With no option and no operand, the shell variables are printed as sorted
assignments. A minus enables an option. A plus disables it. The forms
.BR "set \-o NAME" " and " "set +o NAME"
select the option by long name. With no NAME,
.B set \-o
prints current states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands. The
.B set \-\-options
form prints every option with its state and description. The
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-B
forms control errtrace, history expansion, physical directory resolution,
functrace, and brace expansion. The
.B \-N
form controls the exit code diagnostic that
.B \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
enables at startup, and it reports a successful zero as well. The
.B \-t
form and the
.B onecmd
option stop input after one top-level command. The custom forms
.BR "set \-\-mood" " and " "set \-\-init\-moods"
use the same mood table as the command-line options, and
.B \-M
and
.B \-L
are their short names. Without a value,
.B set \-\-mood
prints the active mood name. The custom form
.B set \-\-tab\-selector
changes the tab selector for the session, and without a value it prints the
active name. A mood change leaves the selector alone. The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-m
letters are accepted without effect. The
.B \-k
form places unquoted assignment arguments in the command environment and
removes them from the argument list. The
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
forms control the diagnostic level. The
.B annoying\-diagnostics
option reports the most pedantic diagnostics. The
.B no\-diagnostics
option skips analysis, and no diagnostic is reported. The
.B extended\-arithmetic
option enables the extended arithmetic operators. The
.B koshkit
option resolves a bundled utility from its bare name.
.PP
The
.B history
option records submitted interactive commands. The
.B histexpand
option expands interactive history references. The supported references are
.BR !! ,
.BR !\-N ,
.BR !N ,
.BR !prefix ,
.BR !?text? ,
and
.BR !# .
An event reference accepts the word designators
.BR :^ ,
.BR :$ ,
.BR :* ,
.BR :N ,
.BR :N\-M ,
.BR :\-M ,
.BR :N* ,
.BR :N\-$ ,
and
.BR :N\- .
The colon may be omitted before
.BR ^ ,
.BR $ ,
.BR * ,
and
.BR % .
The
.B %
designator selects the word matched by the most recent
.B !?text?
search, including a search on an earlier input line.
An event or word reference accepts the pathname modifiers
.BR :h ,
.BR :t ,
.BR :r ,
and
.BR :e .
They select the directory, final component, name without its final suffix, and
final suffix. The
.BI :s/ old / new /
modifier replaces the first literal match.
.B :gs
and
.B :as
replace every match, while
.B :Gs
replaces the first match in each word. The delimiter after
.B s
may be any byte, and its final occurrence may be omitted at the end of the
input line. An unescaped ampersand in the replacement inserts the matched text.
The
.B :&
modifier repeats the previous substitution, including one from an earlier
input line. The
.BR :g& ,
.BR :a& ,
and
.B :G&
forms apply the corresponding scope. An empty old text reuses the previous
substitution text. It reuses the latest
.B !?text?
search text when no previous substitution exists.
.PP
The
.B :q
modifier quotes the selected text as one shell argument. The
.B :x
modifier splits the text at blanks and newlines and quotes each resulting
argument. The
.B :p
modifier prints and records the expanded input without executing it. A leading
.BI ^ old ^ new ^
performs the first substitution on the previous event. The final caret may be
omitted. Text after the final caret remains in the expanded input, and a
modifier may follow it directly.
.PP
Single-quoted and backslash-escaped references remain literal. A reference
immediately before a closing double quote also remains literal. A comment
marker at the start of a word prevents expansion through the end of the line.
An event, word, modifier, or substitution error prevents execution and history
recording and preserves the previous exit status. Both history options are
enabled by default in an interactive shell and disabled in a noninteractive
shell.
.PP
Each shell loads one private history branch at startup. Submitted commands are
appended to the shared file immediately without importing commands written by
other running shells. Recall, expansion, listing, and editing use the private
branch. A command that duplicates the preceding private event is skipped. A
multiline command is stored as one event, and its embedded newlines are
preserved. KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE bounds retained and stored history and defaults to
4096. A value of zero disables history retention and storage. A history file
that exists and cannot be read is reported once at startup, and the session
begins with an empty history.
.PP
The
.B ignoreeof
option requires consecutive end-of-file input before an interactive shell
exits. Its threshold is read from
.BR IGNOREEOF .
Enabling the option sets an unset IGNOREEOF to 10. Assigning IGNOREEOF enables
the option. Disabling the option or unsetting IGNOREEOF disables it. The
.B nolog
option is accepted for Bash compatibility and does not change execution.
.TP
.B "time [\-p|\-\-posix] [\-R] command [argument ...]"
The command or pipeline is timed. The
.B \-p
and
.B \-\-posix
options select the POSIX report. Under the bash mood the report is a blank line
followed by three tab separated rows in the minutes form. The default mood
indents the same three rows and adds a processor busy row. The default report
includes the peak resident set size when the platform supplies a nonzero value.
The
.B \-R
option always adds the same row, including when TIMEFORMAT selects a custom or
empty report. Under the
.B sh
mood the name is not a keyword and is resolved on the command search path.
.TP
.B enable
Every builtin remains enabled. The
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-s
compatibility flags are accepted without effect. The
.B \-a
option lists every builtin.
.TP
.B hash
Resolved command locations are remembered or listed. The
.B \-r
option clears the cache. The
.BI "\-p " path
option records path as the location of each name.
.TP
.B "history [\-cnrwaps] [\-d selector] [operand]"
.br
.B "history {\-S|\-\-sync}"
Without a maintenance option the stored events are listed, and a numeric
operand limits the listing to that many newest events. The
.B \-r
option reads the history file into the list, and an operand names the file that
is read. The
.B \-n
option reads the same way, and an operand names a file whose already read bytes
are skipped. The
.B \-w
option writes the list to the history file, and an operand names a file that is
replaced. Without an operand it replaces the shared file with this shell's
private branch. The
.B \-a
option writes the list to the history file, and an operand names a file that
receives the events this shell has not written to a named file yet. The written
events are bounded by
.BR KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE .
Without an operand,
.B \-a
does nothing because accepted events are already appended immediately.
The
.B \-S
and
.B \-\-sync
forms explicitly replace the private branch with a locked snapshot of the
shared history file and accept no operand.
The
.B \-c
option clears the stored history, and
.B \-p
prints its operands without storing them. The
.B \-d
option deletes an event number, a negative offset from the newest event, or an
inclusive event range. The
.B \-s
option joins its operands with spaces and stores the result as a history event.
.TP
.B "fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]"
The selected history events are edited and executed in the current shell. A
decimal selector names an event number. A negative selector is relative to the
current history position. A text selector finds the newest event with that
prefix. The first and last selectors form an inclusive range. Without
selectors, editing selects the previous event.
.PP
The
.B \-l
option lists events without executing them. A list without selectors contains
up to the previous sixteen events. The
.B \-n
option omits event numbers, and the
.B \-r
option reverses the selected order.
.PP
The editor is selected by
.B \-e
and then by FCEDIT and EDITOR. The default is vi, or ed in POSIX mood. A dash
as the editor skips editing and executes the selected event. The editor receives
the temporary file path as its final argument.
.PP
The form
.B "fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]"
selects one event and applies each literal replacement in order. The result is
printed to standard error and executed. The active fc event is replaced by the
executed command. The return status is the status of the executed command.
.TP
.B bind
Flags and operands are accepted without changing key bindings. The builtin
returns zero.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand, the builtin returns one and prints nothing at top level. It
returns zero inside a function or sourced file. A numeric operand selects a
call frame and prints its line number and source file. A nonnumeric operand is
an error.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern, builtin names are printed one per line. A builtin name
renders the same help as its
.B \-\-help
flag. An unknown name is an error. The
.B \-d
option prints the name and short description. The
.B \-s
option prints only the usage synopsis. The
.B \-m
option prints manpage-style name, synopsis, description, and option sections.
Each mode accepts more than one builtin name.
.TP
.B logout
A login shell runs the EXIT trap and exits. An optional operand supplies the
status. A non-login shell reports an error and returns one.
.TP
.B suspend
The shell stops on SIGSTOP and resumes on SIGCONT. A login shell rejects the
operation unless
.B \-f
is present.
.TP
.BR "declare, typeset"
An indexed array is declared by
.BR \-a ,
an associative array by
.BR \-A ,
an arithmetic integer by
.BR \-i ,
lowercase conversion by
.BR \-l ,
read-only assignment by
.BR \-r ,
uppercase conversion by
.BR \-u ,
and an exported name by
.BR \-x .
The plus forms remove the integer and case-conversion attributes. Lowercase
and uppercase conversion cancel each other when both are requested.
The
.B \-f
and
.B \-F
options restrict the operands to functions, printing each recorded definition or
only its name. The
.B \-p
option prints the matching declarations. The
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-n ,
.B \-t
options are accepted without effect.
.TP
.B local
The name is local to the running function. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.B \-x
attributes have the same meanings as for declare. The
.B \-n
option is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B readonly
Each named variable is marked read-only. The
.B \-p
option lists the read-only variables in a reusable form. The
.B \-a
option treats each name as an indexed array, and
.B \-A
treats each name as an associative array. The
.B \-f
option marks each named function read-only. A read-only function cannot be
redefined and cannot be unset. A bare
.B \-f
lists each read-only function with its body.
.TP
.BR "mapfile, readarray"
The
.B \-t
option reads input lines into an array without their delimiters. The
.B \-n
option limits the line count, and
.B \-s
discards leading lines. The
.B \-O
option selects the first array index. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter with the first byte of its value. The
.B \-u
option reads from a file descriptor. The
.B \-C
option evaluates its callback with the destination index and current record as
arguments. The callback runs after each quantum of records and before the
current record is assigned. The
.B \-c
option sets that quantum. The default quantum is 5000 records.
.TP
.B printf
The
.B \-v
option assigns the formatted result to a variable.
The numeric locale is selected by the first nonempty value of LC_ALL,
LC_NUMERIC, and LANG in every mood. These variables are read from the shell at
each call, and an assignment, an unset, an export, a local, and a prefix
assignment all take effect. A floating operand is read with the decimal point
of that locale, and an operand that is not consumed whole is an invalid number.
The floating conversions print that decimal point. The
.B \(aq
flag groups the digits of the
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR u ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
and
.B G
conversions with the thousands separator of the locale. The C and POSIX
locales, no value, and a locale that the platform cannot select use a dot and
no grouping, and so does every locale on Windows. Arithmetic expansion does
not use the locale.
.TP
.B read
The
.B \-r
option retains backslashes. A plain read treats a trailing backslash as a line
continuation. The
.B \-a
option splits the line into a named indexed array. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter, and an empty value reads to a NUL byte.
The
.B \-n
option bounds the byte count, and
.B \-t
bounds the wait in seconds. The
.B \-p
option prints a prompt at a terminal, and
.B \-s
keeps terminal input unechoed. The
.B \-u
option reads from a file descriptor. The
.B \-q
option reads one byte and returns zero for y or Y. The
.B \-e
option is accepted without effect. The line editor is always used at a
terminal.
.TP
.B compgen
The
.BI "\-V " varname
option stores the candidates in the named indexed array and writes nothing to
standard output. An invalid name is a usage error. An associative or read-only
target is rejected. A generator that matches no candidates leaves the array
empty and returns one. The
.BI "\-P " prefix
and
.BI "\-S " suffix
options add text to each candidate after word prefix matching and
.B \-X
filtering.
.TP
.B type
The
.B \-V
option prints everything the shell holds about each name. A function is reported
with the file and the line its definition starts on, followed by the body as the
shell recorded it. A builtin and a bundled utility are reported with their
description. A keyword and an alias hold nothing further. The
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-P
options keep their terse output and ignore
.BR \-V .
The
.B \-a
option applies the longer form to every resolution it prints. Under the bash
moods a function is named a function and its body is printed from the plain
form. Every other mood names a shell function and prints nothing after it.
.SH KOSHKIT UTILITIES
The
.B koshkit
builtin runs a bundled utility. An unknown utility is an error. Every utility
supports
.BR \-\-help .
A utility invoked through a matching executable name also supports
.BR \-\-version .
The
.B koshkit \-\-list
form prints the utility names one per line.
.PP
With no utility,
.B koshkit
groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings. The Koshka section contains
the additional inspection and reporting utilities. The headings are colored
when standard output is a color terminal.
.PP
The bundled set contains basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown,
cksum, cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env, evil,
evildisk, evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet, evilps, evilss,
expand, expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf, goodcore, goodfsw,
goodnode, goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, link, ln, locale, logger,
logname, ls, make,
man, mkdir, mkfifo, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste, pathchk,
pkill, pr, printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir, sed, seq,
sleep, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee, timeout, touch,
tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink, watch, wc, which, who,
whoami, xargs, and yes.
.PP
The command
.BI "koshkit \-\-assimilate " DIR
installs one symlink per utility into DIR. When direct koshkit lookup is
enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a bundled utility.
.TP
.B basename
The final path component is printed. An optional suffix operand is removed.
.TP
.B cal
A Gregorian month or year is printed. Calendar output starts each week on
Sunday. Month titles and weekday headings are colored on a color terminal.
Weekend dates are red, and the current date is emphasized when it appears.
The
.BR \-a ", " \-\-today
option starts each week on Monday and prints the current date in words below
the calendar.
.TP
.B calc
Arithmetic operands are evaluated. The
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pipe
options read expressions interactively or from a pipe.
Arbitrary precision integers and finite decimal values are accepted in every
mood, and the
.B extended\-arithmetic
option does not narrow a calc result. The abs, atan, ceil, cmp, cos, exp,
fact, fib, floor, frac, gcd, int, iseven, isint, isodd, length, lcm, ln, max,
min, scale, sgn, sin, and sqrt functions are available.
.TP
.B bc
Expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow, input and output
bases, and scale are supported. The
.B \-l
option enables atan, cos, exp, ln, and sin and selects a scale of 20.
.TP
.B cat
Files or standard input are concatenated. The
.B \-n
option numbers lines. The
.B \-u
option is accepted because output is never delayed. The
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
option colors recognized shell source when standard output is a terminal. A
file containing a null byte is written without highlighting. Line numbering
continues across file and standard input boundaries.
.TP
.BR chgrp ", " chown
The group, or the owner and optional group, of each file is changed. The
.B \-R
option recurses, and
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
select how symbolic links are followed. The
.B \-h
option changes a link instead of its target. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option stops a recursion from descending into a directory on a file system
other than the one of its operand. The directory itself is still changed.
.TP
.B chmod
Symbolic or octal modes are applied to each file. The
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
option changes directories and their contents without following symbolic
links. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips every directory below an operand that is on a file system other
than the one of that operand, together with its contents. It has no short form
because
.B \-x
is a mode operand.
.TP
.B cp
Files and directory trees are copied. The
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-v
options are supported. A recursive copy keeps symbolic links as links. The
.B \-L
option follows every symbolic link, and
.B \-H
follows only a link named on the command line. The
.B \-P
option restores the default and copies a link as a link without
.BR \-r .
A copy without
.B \-r
or
.B \-P
follows links. A symbolic link that leads back into a directory being copied is
reported as cyclic and skipped, and the status is 1. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option copies a directory on a file system other than the one of its source
operand as an empty directory, without its contents.
.TP
.B df
Mounted filesystems or the filesystems of the operands are reported. The
.B \-k
and
.B \-P
options select 1024-byte blocks and the POSIX header. The
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
option prints sizes in powers of 1024 with K, M, G, T, and P suffixes under the
header
.BR "Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on" ,
and the
.BR \-H ", " \-\-si
option does the same in powers of 1000, with a lowercase k for the kilo suffix.
.TP
.B diff
Two files are compared line by line. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors removed lines red, added lines green, hunk headers cyan, and the
unified file headers bold. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option
means auto. Output is never colored without the option. The
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-a ,
and repeated
.B \-L
options select unified output, ignore whitespace, force text input, and set the
unified header labels. Up to two labels may be supplied. An unlabeled filesystem
header includes the file modification time. Equal files return zero, different
files return one, and usage or input errors return two.
.TP
.B dirname
The directory portion of one path is printed.
.TP
.B du
Every file and directory below each path is printed in descending size order.
Symbolic links are not followed. Output is colored on a color terminal. The
.B \-s
option prints only each path total. The
.B \-h
option prints human-readable sizes. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips every entry on a file system other than the one of its path
operand. The
.B \-\-tree
option prints the same entries as a tree with box-drawing branches. Children are
sorted by size, largest first, then by name, and each line shows the size
column, the branch, and the last path component, with each path operand shown
as written. With
.BR \-s ,
only the operand lines are printed. The
.BR \-T ", " \-\-top\-largest
option prints a human-readable tree of only the largest entries and every
ancestor of one up to its operand. The tree takes at most 36 lines, or the
terminal height minus four, at least ten, when that is smaller. Names are
colored by file type as in
.BR ls .
With no path, the current directory is used.
.TP
.B env
Temporary assignments are applied before a command, or the environment is
printed. The
.B \-i
option starts with an empty environment, and each
.B \-u
option removes one variable.
.TP
.B xargs
Arguments read from standard input are appended to a command. The
.B \-0
option splits the input on null bytes, and
.B \-r
skips the command when the input is empty. The
.B \-E
option sets the end-of-input marker, and
.B \-I
replaces a string in the command with each input line. The
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-s
options limit each command to a number of lines, arguments, or bytes. The
.B \-p
option asks before each command runs, and
.B \-t
traces each command. The
.B \-x
option fails when a command does not fit in the size limit.
.TP
.B od
Input bytes are written in the formats selected by
.BR \-t .
The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-x
options select the o1, c, u2, o2, d2, and x2 formats in command line order.
.TP
.B printenv
The whole environment is printed when no name is given. Named values are
printed in operand order. The status is one if any name is absent.
.TP
.B file
Filesystem object kinds and file contents are classified. The default content
tests recognize more than 100 executable, archive, document, data, image, audio,
and video formats. The
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-dereference
option classifies a symbolic link. The
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dereference
option follows it. The
.BR \-i ", " \-\-regular\-only
option skips content tests. The
.BR \-m ", " \-\-magic\-file
option adds a magic database, and
.BR \-M ", " \-\-magic\-only
uses only the named databases.
.TP
.B find
Sorted trees are walked with
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-iname ,
.BR \-type ,
.BR \-maxdepth ,
.BR \-mindepth ,
.BR \-print ,
and
.BR \-print0 .
The
.BR \-xdev " and " \-mount
primaries are always true and keep the walk from descending into a directory on
a file system other than the one of its starting point. The directory itself is
still matched. The
.B \-exec
action runs a command that ends at a
.B ;
argument for each matched path, replacing each
.B {}
with the path. A command ended by
.B +
after
.B {}
receives the matched paths in batches. A failed command skips the later actions
for that path.
.TP
.B flock
A command runs while a directory lock is held. The
.B \-\-transaction\-held\-lock
option keeps the lock after the process that launched the transaction exits.
.TP
.B fuser
Process IDs that use each named file are printed. The
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-u
options are supported.
.TP
.B getconf
System and string variables take one name. Path variables also take a path.
An undefined or unsupported variable prints
.BR undefined .
A platform query failure prints a diagnostic and returns a nonzero status. The
.BR \-a ,
.B \-\-all
option prints every known variable as a name and value, using the current
directory for path variables unless a path is supplied. The
.BR \-v ,
.B \-\-specification
option selects a supported POSIX environment.
.TP
.B grep
Regular-expression matches are printed. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors matches bold red, file names magenta, line numbers green, and
separators cyan. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option means auto.
When color is on, GREP_COLORS holds colon-separated GNU capabilities such as
.BR ms=01;31 ,
with ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv taking effect and an unknown name
ignored. Parsing stops at the first character of a value that is not a digit or
a semicolon, and an empty value turns that color off. The deprecated GREP_COLOR
sets the match color below GREP_COLORS. Matches are not highlighted with
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-l ,
or
.BR \-q .
Output is never colored without the option. The
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.BR \-h
options ignore case, invert matches, recurse into directories, show line
numbers, and suppress file-name prefixes. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option makes
.B \-r
skip every directory on a file system other than the one of its operand. It has
no short form because
.B \-x
matches whole lines. The combined
.B \-rnh
form is accepted, and options may follow the operands.
A pattern is a basic regular expression. The
.B \-E
option reads patterns as extended regular expressions and the
.B \-F
option reads them as fixed strings. The two options conflict and exit with
status 2.
Each
.B \-e
option adds a pattern, and each
.B \-f
option adds the lines of a file as patterns. When either option is given, every
operand is a file. A newline inside a pattern separates two patterns, and a line
is selected when any pattern matches it. An empty pattern matches every line, and an
empty pattern file matches none.
The
.B \-x
option selects only lines that match entirely.
The
.B \-c
option prints the count of selected lines for each file, prefixed with the file
name when several files are searched. The
.B \-l
option prints each file with a selected line once, and takes precedence over
.BR \-c .
The
.B \-q
option prints nothing and exits with status 0 at the first selected line, even
if an earlier file could not be read. The
.B \-s
option suppresses messages about files that cannot be read, and the exit status
is still 2. The standard input is named
.I (standard input)
in prefixes, counts, and lists. The exit status is 0 when a line is selected, 1
when no line is selected, and 2 on an error. The locale is chosen from the first
nonempty value of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG .
In a UTF-8 locale, a dot and a bracket expression match one whole character.
In every other locale, they match one byte.
.TP
.B head
The first ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
option selects a line count and the
.B \-c
option selects a byte count.
.TP
.B tail
The last ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
option selects a line count and the
.B \-c
option selects a byte count. The
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and
.B \-\-silent
options never print file name headers, and the
.BR \-v ,
.B \-\-verbose
options always print them. The
.B \-f
option keeps each regular file open after the initial output and prints the
bytes appended to it, with a header whenever the output switches to another
file. The
.B \-F
option follows the file name, reports and reopens a file that is replaced,
removed, or created later, and implies
.BR \-\-retry .
The
.BI \-\-follow= descriptor|name
option selects the mode, and a name follow without
.B \-\-retry
stops following a removed file. A truncated file is reported on standard error
and read again from its start. Standard input is followed only when it is a
regular file, and a pipe is printed once. The
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-sleep\-interval
option sets the longest wait between checks in seconds, and the wait is one
second by default. The
.BI \-\-pid= pid
option ends the follow after that process exits. On Linux and macOS, the
follow is woken by file changes, and on other targets, the files are checked
every interval. An interrupted follow ends with status 130.
.TP
.B killall
Processes are signaled by exact name. The signal is selected and the signal
names are listed with
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B pkill
Processes whose names match a pattern are signaled. The signal is selected and
the signal names are listed with
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B ln
Hard links are created by default. The
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
options create a symbolic link, remove an existing destination first, follow a
symbolic link source, and link a symbolic link source itself.
.TP
.B logname
The login session user name is printed. It can differ from the effective user
reported by
.BR whoami .
.TP
.B ls
Sorted entries are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-h .
The
.B \-F
option appends a type indicator to each name, and
.B \-p
appends a slash to each directory. The
.B \-d
option lists a directory operand as itself. The
.B \-n
option prints numeric owner and group identifiers, and
.B \-g
and
.B \-o
print the long form without the owner or without the group. The
.B \-k
option is accepted because sizes are never scaled. The
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-r
options sort by modification time, by size, and in reverse. The
.B \-R
option lists every subdirectory that is reached, and
.B \-\-tree
prints an indented directory tree. The
.B \-L
option sets the maximum recursion depth. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option keeps
.B \-R
and
.B \-\-tree
from descending into a directory on a file system other than the one of its
operand, and the directory is still listed. It has no short form because
.B \-x
lists entries across. Names are colored by file
type when the standard output is a terminal. The
.B \-\-color
option accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B make
Compact make rules are run. The
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-C ,
and
.B \-\-directory
options are supported. The
.B \-B
option always rebuilds targets, and
.B \-k
continues independent goals after an error. The
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-t
options provide the POSIX make execution modes.
The
.B \-j
extension accepts an optional decimal job count and propagates it through
MAKEFLAGS.
Common variable functions include
.BR call ,
.BR eval ,
.BR filter ,
.BR filter-out ,
.BR foreach ,
.BR patsubst ,
.BR shell ,
.BR sort ,
and
.BR wildcard .
.TP
.B mkdir
Directories are created with
.B \-p
and an octal or symbolic
.B \-m
mode. A symbolic mode is applied to mode 777 with the chmod grammar.
.TP
.B mknod
FIFO, character, and block special files are created with POSIX type operands
or the modern
.B \-\-type
,
.B \-\-fifo
,
.B \-\-character
,
and
.B \-\-block
options. Device nodes accept
.B \-\-major
and
.B \-\-minor
numbers; the
.B \-m
option sets the permission mode.
.TP
.B mv
Sources are renamed with
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B nproc
The available logical processor count is printed. The
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-ignore=COUNT
options are supported.
.TP
.B ps
Processes are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.BR \-x .
The
.B \-w
option is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B readlink
The target of each symbolic link is printed on its own line. A file that is not
a link is reported, the remaining files are still read, and the status is 1.
The
.B \-n
option suppresses the trailing newline of a single file and is ignored for
several.
.TP
.B realpath
The canonical absolute form of each path is printed.
.TP
.B rm
Paths are removed with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
Root, dot, and dot-dot are refused. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips a directory on a file system other than the one of its path
operand, reports it as being on a different device, and makes the status 1.
.TP
.B rmdir
Empty directories are removed. The
.B \-p
option also removes empty parent directories.
.TP
.B seq
Signed integer sequences are printed with a nonzero step.
.TP
.B sleep
Execution pauses for summed durations with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.B sort
Input lines are sorted in byte order, or by the keys named with
.BR \-k .
A key is
.IR F[.C][bdfinr][,F[.C][bdfinr]] .
The
.BR \-t
option sets the field separator byte. The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-r
options ignore leading blanks, compare only blanks and alphanumeric characters,
fold lowercase letters, compare only printable characters, compare leading
numbers by value, and reverse the result. The
.B \-u
option writes the first of each run of equal keys. The
.B \-c
option checks that the input is sorted and reports the first line out of
order, and
.B \-C
checks it without a message. The
.B \-o
option writes the result to a file after all input is read. The
.B \-s
option skips the last-resort comparison of whole lines, and
.B \-m
is accepted for merging sorted input.
.TP
.B stty
Terminal settings are reported or changed. The
.B \-a
option reports all settings. The
.B \-g
option reports an encoded state that can be supplied later to restore the
same state.
.TP
.B tee
Standard input is copied to output files. The
.B \-a
option appends.
.TP
.B timeout
A command runs with a time limit. The duration accepts a suffix. The
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-\-kill\-after ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-\-preserve\-status
options select the signal, send KILL after a further duration, and return the
command status.
.TP
.B touch
File times are updated. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-t
options select the access time, suppress creation of a missing file, select the
modification time, name a reference file, and set an explicit timestamp.
.TP
.B tr
Input bytes are translated with ranges and character classes. The
.B \-d
option deletes bytes. The
.BR \-c
and
.B \-C
options replace the first set with every byte outside it, in byte order. The
.B \-s
option squeezes each run of a repeated byte from the last set into one byte.
.TP
.B uniq
Adjacent equal lines are collapsed. The
.B \-c
option prints run counts. The
.B \-d
and
.B \-u
options write only lines that have a repeat or only lines that have none. The
.B \-f
and
.B \-s
options ignore leading fields and characters when lines are compared. A second
operand is the output file.
.TP
.B unlink
One non-directory path is removed.
.TP
.B evil
System identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap, root filesystem, process,
filesystem, and network information is printed in titled sections named
Identity, Session, Accounts, Resources, Limits, Activity, and Anomalies, each
with its name and value rows indented two spaces beneath the title. A section
without rows is omitted. The
.B \-a
option adds system details that are omitted from the default report. The
additional activity counters are shown only when the platform provides them;
pressure-stall totals are measured in microseconds. On Linux, this includes
counts of the current process's namespace entries and cgroup memberships;
each reports unavailable if its procfs source cannot be read. The
.B \-s
option selects identity fields. The
.B \-u
option prints every local user account. The
.B \-\-color
option accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B goodcore
A running process can be captured with
.BR \-p .
An existing core can be packaged with its executable through
.BR \-b .
Mapped libraries and host metadata are included. Zstandard compression is used
when available, with gzip as the fallback.
.TP
.B eviliso
Isolation evidence is grouped into namespace, cgroup, login-session, remote
socket, container-runtime, container, and Kubernetes sections. With no
selector, all sections are shown. The
.B -a
or
.B --all
option includes detailed rows, including per-process namespace attribution,
controller membership, login times, and remote peers. The
.B namespace
section groups visible process relationships by namespace type and numeric
identifier and prints their process counts. With
.BR --all ,
it prints type, identifier, process ID, process name, and self/other role for
each relationship. A relationship that cannot be read or disappears during
collection is retained with an
.B unavailable
identifier. The
.B cgroup
section prints hierarchy, controller, path, process ID, process name, and
self/other role columns. With
.BR --all ,
it adds each membership of another process that has the same hierarchy,
controller, and path as one of the current process's memberships. Sessions are
printed as user and terminal rows. With
.BR --all ,
each row also includes its login time; an unavailable timestamp is shown
explicitly. The
.B \-\-remote
option selects a remote-peer table with protocol, state, queues, local and peer
endpoints, and socket identifiers. With
.B \-\-all ,
the same rows add process, owner, network namespace, orchestrator, runtime,
container, and cgroup columns. Command lines, process names, and cgroup
memberships are shortened to keep the table usable. A dash marks information
that disappeared or could not be read.
Otherwise
.BR -n , -c , -s ,
and
.B -r
select namespaces, cgroups, sessions, and remote sockets; the
.B \-k
option selects container-runtime evidence. The long-only
.B \-\-container
and
.B \-\-kubernetes
selectors select only their corresponding sections.
The plural
.B \-\-containers
spelling is an equivalent alias for
.BR \-\-container .
Selectors combine, and only the selected sections are printed.
The remote section starts with a socket summary of remote and total socket
counts.
.PP
The runtime section prints runtime, source, and evidence columns. Evidence is
a cgroup path or an existing container marker file such as
.BR /.dockerenv .
With
.BR \-\-all ,
it adds process ID, process name, and self, other, or host role columns.
The container section prints runtime, container identifier, and process count
columns. With
.BR \-\-all ,
each process has its own row with process ID, name, role, and cgroup columns
in place of the count. The Kubernetes section prints an evidence table with
source, host, namespace, and evidence columns. Evidence comes from the
.B KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST
variable, the service-account namespace file, and
.B kubepods
cgroup components. Values read from the environment or the file are limited to
one line of 256 bytes with control bytes replaced by spaces. A second table
lists workloads with pod UID, QoS class, runtime, container, and process count
columns. The QoS class is
.BR guaranteed ,
.BR burstable ,
or
.BR besteffort .
With
.BR \-\-all ,
the process count is replaced by process ID, name, role, and cgroup columns for
each process.
.TP
.B evildisk
The utility prints capacity and filesystem identity for each named path or
mounted filesystem. It also prints native disk error and retry totals when the
operating system provides them. With
.B \-a
mount labels and UUIDs plus filesystem error counters are included when the
platform exposes them. The same option adds SMART status, model, and protocol
data when smartctl or macOS diskutil is available.
.TP
.B evilfiles
Open files are listed for visible processes in a table titled Open files whose
rows are indented two spaces beneath the title. The
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.B \-t
options filter by process, owner, command prefix, socket descriptor, and terse
process identifier output. On a terminal the command and name columns are
shortened with an ellipsis to fit the width; the
.BR \-w " and " \-\-wide
options keep them whole. Pipes show the FIFO type, and anonymous inodes show
the a_inode type. On Linux a socket shows its family as unix, IPv4, IPv6,
netlink, or packet, and the ENDPOINT column holds the local and remote address
of a TCP or UDP socket, with the TCP state, and the path and peer inode of a
Unix-domain socket.
.TP
.B evilfs
Mounted filesystem sources, targets, types, and options are printed in a sorted
table.
.TP
.B evilio
Current memory, swap, and cumulative disk I/O counters are printed without
waiting. The
.B \-a
option adds processor usage, paging, scheduler state, pressure stalls, disk
activity, and swap rates from a one second sample.
Nonzero stall rates and disk failures are highlighted. Byte sizes and byte
rates use compact binary units by default; the
.BR \-h " and " \-\-human-readable
options request that representation explicitly. The
.B \-\-cumulative
option waits for the specified number of seconds, M, and prints the counter
change over that window; the default window is one second. With
.BR \-\-ps ,
each mode prints per-process data. The
.BR \-l " and " \-\-live
options print the report again and again until interrupted. Their optional
value, N, is the number of seconds between samples and between refreshes; it
defaults to 0.5 seconds.
Output uses
the alternate screen and restores the previous contents on exit.
Every frame of a live utility starts with a header line that names the utility,
shows a LIVE indicator, the local time, the window and the refresh
interval, and, on a terminal, the keys the utility accepts. The header
is shortened to fit the terminal width and is colored only when color is
enabled. The terminal reads keys without echo and without Enter: q quits with
status 0, and an interrupt quits with status 130. Each frame is written with one
write and follows the terminal size at the time of the frame. Output
that is not a terminal carries a plain header and no escape sequences.
When both options are present,
.B \-\-live
sets how often the utility samples and refreshes, and
.B \-\-cumulative
sets the length of a rolling window that keeps the counter changes of the last
M seconds. The window never changes the sampling or refresh interval. The
counter columns show the accumulated change inside the window, and a heading
such as
.B READ OPS/2s
names its length. A burst stays in the report until its samples age out of the
window, and then it leaves at once; counters do not fade gradually. When the
window is shorter than the interval, the last interval is divided in
proportion. A row that is not seen again leaves the report after it has been
missing for a whole window. A process is identified by its identifier and
start time, so a reused identifier begins a new row. The
.BR \-\-ps ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-NUMBER
options otherwise print only lifetime process I/O counters. They show every
process, limit the table, select one process, or limit the table to NUMBER rows.
The
.B \-\-sort
option orders rows by a full metric name or its shortest unambiguous prefix.
Process reports accept
.BR pid ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR read-ops ,
and
.BR write-ops .
Disk reports accept
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR read-ops ,
.BR write-ops ,
.BR busy ,
.BR read-latency ,
.BR write-latency ,
.BR average-queue ,
.BR queue ,
.BR errors ,
and
.BR retries .
Metrics sort from largest to smallest, unavailable values sort last, and ties
use the process identifier or device name. Process identifiers sort from
smallest to largest. Busy, latency, and average queue values require a sampled
report. Live reports sort the values from the displayed window.
.TP
.B evillogs
Core dump settings, recognized dump reports, and known log directories are
printed. Each core dump directory and each log directory is a titled table
with its name, entry count, and total size in the title and its rows indented
two spaces beneath it. The
.BR \-\-cores
and
.B \-\-logs
options select one report.
.TP
.B evilnet
Assigned IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are printed in a sorted table. The
.B \-a
option adds interface traffic, packet errors, packet drops, and a TCP counter
table.
.B \-f
prints TCP failure and packet-loss telemetry without interface or socket rows.
Nonzero error, drop, and failure counters are also reported as warnings. The
.BR \-t " and " \-\-traffic
options print only the interface traffic table. On Linux the interface that
carries the default IPv4 route is shown in bold green when color is enabled.
The
.B \-\-live
option samples and refreshes the traffic table every N seconds, defaulting to
0.5 seconds.
The
.B \-\-cumulative
option sets a window of M seconds, defaulting to one second. Without
.BR \-\-live ,
it enables the traffic table, waits M seconds, and prints the counter change
over that window. With
.B \-\-live
it keeps a rolling window of M seconds that never changes the sampling and
refresh interval. The traffic columns show the accumulated change inside the
window, and the headings carry its length, as in
.BR RX\ PACKETS/2s .
The
.B \-\-sort
option orders traffic rows by name, receive or transmit bytes, packet counts,
errors, or drops, using the shortest unambiguous prefix. Numeric counters sort
from largest to smallest, unavailable values sort last, and ties use the
interface name.
.TP
.B goodnode
Inode identity, allocation, known filesystem capabilities, verification state,
and a CRC32C checksum are printed for each path. The
.B \-i
option locates an inode beneath the path selected by
.BR \-r .
The
.B \-\-verify
option runs bounded native filesystem verification when the platform supports
it. On APFS it runs
.B diskutil verifyVolume
and reports the command status without parsing localized output.
.TP
.B evilps
Visible processes are printed as a parent and child tree. The
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-U ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.BR \-w
options show process identifiers, sort numerically, enable every field, show
command lines, show owners, show processor usage, show resident memory, and
keep command lines at full length. Without
.BR \-w ,
a command line is cut to the width of the terminal. Processor usage is
accumulated time in an ordinary report and the average utilization over the
window in a sampled report. The
.B \-\-sort
option accepts name, pid, cpu, memory, or an unambiguous prefix of one of those
names. Each sorted tree starts with its highest-ranked process and shows its
parents and children beneath it. CPU sorting uses the value shown in the report.
The
.B \-NUMBER
operand limits the output to NUMBER processes.
On a terminal, in a report and in a live view, command lines are shortened to
the width of the output; the
.BR \-w " and " \-\-wide
options keep them whole.
The
.B \-C
option is shorthand for
.BR \-\-cumulative .
.BR \-l " and " \-\-live
options sample and refresh the process tree until interrupted. Their optional
value, N, is the number of seconds between samples and between refreshes; it
defaults to 0.5 seconds. On a terminal,
.BR j ,
.BR k ,
and the arrow keys scroll, the page keys scroll by ten lines,
.B g
and
.B G
jump to the top and bottom, and
.B s
cycles the sort key. The
.B /
key starts a search; the typed text is edited with Backspace, Enter applies it,
and Escape clears it. The
.B \-\-cumulative
option sets a window of M seconds, defaulting to one second. With
.BR \-\-live ,
the window rolls over the last M seconds and never changes the sampling or
refresh interval; a window shorter than the interval divides the last interval
in proportion. A new process shows unavailable processor utilization until two
samples have been collected. A process is identified by its identifier and
start time, so a reused identifier begins a new row. Without
.BR \-\-live ,
the utility takes two snapshots M seconds apart and the report shows the
average processor utilization between them.
.TP
.B evilss
Visible TCP, UDP, and Unix-domain sockets are printed in a table. Address
family, protocol, listening state, process identifiers, and the header can be
selected with ss-compatible options. The
.B \-\-live
option, which has no short form because
.B \-l
selects listening sockets, reads the sockets again and refreshes the same table
every optional number of seconds until interrupted; the default is 0.5 seconds.
.TP
.B goodstat
File metadata is printed as a labeled report. Its type uses the same classifier
as
.BR "koshkit file" .
The
.B \-L
option follows symbolic links.
The
.B \-c
and
.B \-f
options add a CRC32C checksum and filesystem capacity report, respectively.
.TP
.B goodfsw
Path changes are reported by polling filesystem metadata. Machine-readable
output is the default: each record contains the Unix scan time, a numeric event
mask, and the path. The
.BR \-h " and " \-\-human-readable
options use formatted timestamps and descriptive event names. The
.BR \-m " and " \-\-machine-readable
options select the numeric format explicitly. The
.B \-\-timezone
option selects local or UTC time for human output, and
.B \-\-precision
selects zero through nine fractional timestamp digits. The
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-\-event-flags ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.B \-e
options select recursion, timestamp prefixes, appended event names, one
filesystem, one event batch, the polling period, and an excluded substring.
.TP
.B retry
A command is repeated until it succeeds or its attempt limit is reached. The
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.B \-q
options set the attempt count, initial delay, backoff factor, maximum delay,
and quiet mode.
.TP
.B stat
File or filesystem metadata is printed. The
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-\-printf
options dereference links, select filesystem data, select terse output, apply a
format, and apply a format without an automatic newline.
.TP
.B sync
Dirty filesystem data is flushed. The
.B \-d
option flushes file data for each operand. The
.B \-f
option flushes the filesystem containing each operand.
.TP
.B watch
A command is run at a fixed interval. The
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-g ,
.B \-e
options set the interval, omit the title, exit after output changes, and exit
after a command error. The
.B \-x
option is accepted for compatibility. The title and screen controls are emitted
only when standard output is a terminal.
.PP
Utilities with styled reports use color when their output stream is a terminal.
Redirected output contains no terminal escape sequences.
.TP
.B wc
Lines, words, characters, and bytes are counted with
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.BR \-c .
A character is a UTF-8 sequence.
.TP
.B which
Builtins, PATH programs, and koshkit utilities are resolved. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-q ,
and
.B \-\-quiet
options are supported.
.TP
.B whoami
The effective user name is printed.
.TP
.B yes
The joined operands, or y when none are given, are repeated until output
closes.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode. The command
.B set \-o vi
selects vi mode. The command
.B set \-o emacs
restores emacs mode.
.PP
When a ghost suggestion is shown at the end of the line, the right arrow and
End accept all of it. Ctrl\-Right and Alt\-F accept only its next word.
.PP
Ctrl\-K, Ctrl\-U, Ctrl\-W, Alt\-D, Alt\-Backspace and Ctrl\-Delete save the
text they erase in a kill ring of 16 entries that lasts for the session, and
consecutive kills join into one entry. Ctrl\-Y inserts the newest entry. Alt\-Y
right after a yank replaces the inserted text with the next older entry and
wraps around after the oldest. Ctrl\-T swaps the character before the cursor
with the one under it, or the last two characters at the end of the line. Alt\-T
swaps the word before the cursor with the word after it, or the last two words
at the end of the line. Alt\-. and Alt\-_ insert the last word of the newest
history entry, and each further press replaces it with the last word of an
older entry. A quoted last word is inserted whole with its quotes. Undo reverts
a yank or a last-word insertion with all of its replacements in one step.
.PP
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E opens the line in a temporary file with the editor that VISUAL
names, else EDITOR, else vi. The value is split on blanks without expansion.
When the editor exits with status 0, the saved text replaces the line with its
trailing newlines removed, and nothing runs until Enter is pressed. An editor
that is not found or that exits with another status leaves the line alone and
reports a message above the prompt. The key does nothing under
.BR \-T .
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-U undoes the last edit.
.PP
On a non-empty line, the up arrow recalls only the history entries that begin
with the text typed before the first up arrow. Each further up arrow moves to
an older match and the down arrow moves back. Stepping past the newest match
restores the typed text. An empty line steps through the whole history. The
.B set \+o history-prefix-search
command turns this off, and the option is on by default.
.PP
Once the cursor is past the command word, the row directly below the input
shows a dimmed hint for the command. The hint is the synopsis of a builtin, of
a bundled koshkit utility, or of an external program, and it becomes the
description of the flag under the cursor when the cursor is inside a flag. The
row is cut with an ellipsis at the terminal width, never wraps, and is hidden
while a completion menu or history search is open and erased when the line is
submitted. The hint reads only data already loaded, and it never runs
.BR man
or the command. The synopsis and flag descriptions of an external program
appear after a Tab completion of its flags has loaded its manual page or help
output. The
.B set \+o inline-hints
command turns the hint row off, and the option is on by default.
.PP
When the line has a syntax problem that running it would report, the same row
shows that problem in place of the synopsis, in the same dim style. The line is
parsed as Enter would parse it in the current mood, and the row shows the first
parse error followed by its detail, such as
.BR "Unterminated if, expected 'fi'" .
This covers every error the parser reports, among them an unterminated quote,
an unclosed
.BR $( ,
.BR $(( ,
.BR ${ ,
.BR <( ,
backtick, subshell, brace group, array or
.BR [[ ,
an
.BR if ,
loop, case or function with no closing keyword, a stray
.BR ) ,
.BR ;; ,
.BR fi ,
.BR done ,
.B esac
or
.BR then ,
a pipe or
.BR && ,
.B ||
or
.B |&
with no command on one side, a redirection with no target, a
.B <<
with no delimiter, a
.B case
with no
.BR in ,
a
.B function
with no name, a bad
.B for
variable, and an empty subshell, brace group or loop body. The editor submits
the whole buffer on Enter and has no continuation prompt, so an open construct
is an error even when a later line would close it, except after a trailing
backslash, which joins the next row and shows nothing. While the caret ends a
word where the error starts, as in a
.B fi
that may still become
.BR find ,
the row waits for the next key. A line over 2048 bytes shows nothing. The row
follows
.BR inline-hints .
.PP
The
.B set \-o auto-pair
command makes a typed
.BR ( ,
.BR [ ,
.BR { ,
double quote, or single quote insert its closer after the caret. The closer
is added only at the line end or before a blank or a closing bracket, never
after a backslash, and a quote is left single after a letter, a digit, or the
same quote. Typing the closer over an inserted one steps over it, and
Backspace between an empty inserted pair deletes both. Any other key, such as
an arrow, keeps the inserted closers as plain text. The option is off by
default.
.PP
The interactive terminal title shows the current user and directory. While a
foreground program runs, the title shows its command line. Output from
PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can replace the idle title.
.PP
RPS1, or RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt. It is expanded
with PS1 before each prompt and drawn right-aligned on the first input row,
ending one column short of the right edge. Escape sequences take no columns.
The right prompt is hidden while the text and ghost on that row would come
within one column of it, while the terminal is too narrow for it, while PS1 is
cut to fit the row, and when its expansion holds a line break. Rows after the
first input row never carry it. It is never part of the submitted line, and
without
.B transient\-prompt
it stays in the scrollback after Enter.
.PP
The
.B set \-o transient\-prompt
command makes Enter redraw the submitted line after a short prompt so the
scrollback stays compact. Everything from the first row of PS1 down is erased,
including the right prompt, the ghost, the hint row, and any menu rows, and the
line is drawn again after the expansion of PS1_TRANSIENT. When PS1_TRANSIENT is
unset or empty, the short prompt is "$ ", or "# " for root, the expansion of
the \\$ escape and a space. The line keeps its highlighting, and a multi-row
PS1 collapses to the short prompt. Ctrl\-C and the other keys that end a line
leave it as drawn. The option is off by default.
.PP
Ghost text uses prefix matches from completion and history. Tab also offers
smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command position includes
keywords, builtins, functions, aliases, koshkit utilities, and programs from
PATH. An exact prefix ranks before a subsequence match.
.PP
An explicit Tab first replaces the token with the longest common prefix. When
that prefix does not extend the token and more than one candidate remains, the
candidates are presented by the selector that
.B \-\-tab\-selector
names. Ctrl\-Space acts as Tab on a POSIX system.
The optional
.B set \-o space-after-completion
setting inserts one space after a complete non-directory candidate is accepted;
it is disabled by default.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu of the candidates below the line. The
completion menu starts under the token being completed, and starts at the left
edge when its widest row, the description column included, does not fit to the
right of that column, or when an item of its first row would be cut there. The
history
menu always starts at the left edge. The
down arrow moves to the next row, the up arrow moves back, and Tab accepts the
highlighted row. In the completion menu, Escape closes the menu with the current
edit, and Ctrl\-G restores the line that opened it. Enter closes the completion
menu and runs the line as it stands. The highlighted row is not accepted. A first
row names the active source and shows the available keys beside it, reading
.B selecting completions
for the candidates of a token and
.B incremental history search
for a stable snapshot of the shared history file. Opening the search does not
replace or merge the session's private history. The terminal height bounds the
number of rows, and a count row names the part that is drawn. A first row too
long for the width wraps onto further rows between its items, each starting at
the column of the menu rows.
.PP
A printable key is inserted into the token, and the menu is drawn again from the
candidates that still match. A candidate matches when the token opens it, when
the token appears inside it whole, or when the bytes of the token appear inside
it in order. The three kinds are drawn in that order, and each kind keeps the
order the candidates arrived in. Letters are compared without case. Narrowing
filters the most recent search results. The file system and history are not
searched again. Backspace erases the last character of the token
and widens the list again. A search that matches nothing keeps the menu open on
a dimmed row reading
.BR "no matches, erase to widen the search" ,
and a backspace brings the list back. An accepted candidate that names a
directory opens a menu of the entries it holds. The menu closes when any other
key is pressed. That key then acts on the line.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to the program named by
.B KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND
and its output replaces the token. Raw mode is left while the selector program
runs, and the prompt is repainted afterwards. A selector that reports no
match or is dismissed leaves the line alone and prints nothing. A single
remaining candidate keeps the plain listing. A session without a controlling
terminal keeps it too.
.PP
An empty
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ,
a name that resolves to no program, and a selector that exits with a status
other than 0, 1, or 130 each report a message above the prompt and keep the
plain listing.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates below the prompt as a static column
list. The list starts under the token being completed, and starts at the left
edge when its widest candidate, and its widest description when it shows any,
does not fit to the right of that column. A terminal without decoration support always receives the plain listing.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the same selector. The line is the search
query, and the entries it matches are offered by the same three kinds, with the
newest first inside each kind. The interactive selector draws them in the menu.
Each offered entry is drawn with the syntax colors the line itself receives. The
selected row is drawn plain under its reverse video. Typing narrows the list,
and an accepted row replaces the whole line. The external selector receives the
same entries, and the line it prints replaces the whole line. The plain selector
keeps the incremental search inside the editor and accepts the same keys as the
menu. The down arrow and Ctrl\-R step back to an older match, the up arrow
and Ctrl\-F step forward to a newer one, Enter or Tab accepts the match without
running it, and Escape or Ctrl\-G cancels the search and restores the preceding
line state. A history that matches nothing leaves the line alone.
.PP
Completion identifies the command segment at the cursor across quoted
separators, command substitutions, and here-document bodies. Directory prefixes
that use quotes, escapes, variables, or tildes retain their typed form. An
explicit Tab at command position offers executable files and directories.
Syntax highlighting distinguishes syntax, commands, flags, variables, paths,
globs, and invalid input.
.PP
When the caret is on a parenthesis, bracket, or brace, or just after one, that
bracket and its partner are drawn bold on a bright background. A caret on the
dollar sign of
.B $(
or
.B ${
counts as a caret on its bracket. The doubled forms
.BR [[ ,
.BR ]] ,
.BR ((
and
.B ))
are marked whole. A bracket inside quotes, a comment, or a here-document body,
or one escaped with a backslash, never pairs. An unmatched bracket keeps its
usual color.
.PP
Each vi line begins in insert mode. Escape enters command mode. Command mode
supports motions, counted operators, named registers, change repeat, and visual
selection. The
.B v
key selects characters. The
.B V
key selects whole lines.
.PP
The
.B u
key undoes the last edit in command mode. Ctrl\-_ undoes it in either mode.
Ctrl\-^ redoes the edit in either mode. Ctrl\-R redoes it in command mode.
Ctrl\-R opens the history search in insert mode. The slash key opens the same
search in command mode.
.PP
The colon key opens an ex command. The commands q, q!, wq, wq!, x, and quit
exit the shell. The cursor is a bar in insert mode, a block in command mode,
and an underline in visual mode or while an operator awaits a motion.
.PP
In command mode, arrow keys move between buffer rows without recalling history.
Insert-mode arrow keys recall history. With an operator pending, Up or Down
operates on the current line and the adjacent line. The dk and dj commands
perform the corresponding linewise operations.
.PP
Ctrl\-V enters rectangular selection in vi command mode. The d and x keys delete
the rectangle. The I, c, and C keys insert text at the left column of every
selected line.
.PP
Ctrl\-V enters multi\-cursor mode in emacs mode. Up and Down extend the span. A
typed byte is inserted at every cursor. Backspace erases at every cursor.
Ctrl\-G leaves the mode.
.SS Terminal shell integration
An interactive shell whose standard output is a terminal writes semantic marks
that terminals read to find prompts, commands, exit statuses, and the working
directory. Every mark ends with BEL. Each prompt is preceded by the end of the
previous command as ESC ] 133 ; D ; status, the working directory as
ESC ] 7 ; file://host/path, and the prompt start as ESC ] 133 ; A.
The prompt ends with ESC ] 133 ; B. A submitted command is announced by
ESC ] 133 ; C after PS0 is printed. The first prompt carries no D mark.
The marks occupy no columns in the prompt width. Under
.BR transient\-prompt ,
the short prompt drawn over a submitted line starts on the row the A mark
named and ends with its own ESC ] 133 ; B, so the terminal finds the command
after it. When TERM_PROGRAM is vscode,
the shell also writes ESC ] 633 ; P ; Cwd= and ESC ] 633 ; E ; command line for
VS Code. No mark is written when standard output is not a terminal, when TERM
is dumb, or when
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
is 0.
.SH JOB CONTROL
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job. POSIX stages share
one process group. The final stage determines $!, the ordinary pipeline status,
and the primary job identity. With pipefail, the rightmost failing stage may
determine the status.
.PP
A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the final one. The
.B wait
builtin returns that stored status without waiting for the process again. The
.B fg
and
.B bg
builtins resume every stage of the job.
.PP
A coprocess is one asynchronous job and sets $!. Its descriptors are numbered
above 9 and are closed across an exec. A subshell receives neither descriptor,
while NAME and NAME_PID stay readable there.
.SH SIGNALS
The main shell ignores SIGPIPE. A forked child restores the default SIGPIPE
action. A producer terminated by SIGPIPE therefore returns status 141.
.PP
On Windows, a background process runs in its own console process group.
.PP
The CHLD trap runs its action once for every child the shell reaps, at the next
command boundary. Under
.B set -m
a job that stops counts as one arrival of its own, and a job that is continued
counts as none. No child signal is raised on Windows, and the reap alone
reaches that boundary. The Windows
.B kill
builtin sends only KILL and TERM. A script there cannot raise CHLD by hand. A
CHLD action that returns from the function it fired inside leaves the condition
installed, and the action fires again for the next child the shell reaps.
.PP
A trapped signal that arrives while
.B wait
blocks ends the wait. The action runs at the boundary the builtin returns to,
and
.B wait
reports 128 plus the signal number. The job it was waiting on is left running,
and the operands it has not reached are abandoned. A child arrival leaves the
wait blocked.
.PP
A redirection whose open blocks, such as a named pipe with no peer, resumes
after a trapped signal runs its action. The file is still opened and the
command runs against it. An untrapped interrupt ends the open, and the shell
reports the interrupt against the redirection.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors, warnings, and analysis diagnostics include source locations when a
mapping exists. A diagnostic may include a source line, caret, detail location,
and trailing note. A quoted source line is syntax colored and clipped to the
terminal width.
.PP
The analysis stage follows readable static source operands recursively. Dynamic
and unavailable operands are skipped.
.PP
An analysis diagnostic may include a ShellCheck identifier. A native analysis
diagnostic carries no identifier.
.PP
In the default mood, strict and lenient findings reject the script. Annoying
findings remain warnings. The annoying tier contains additional safety and style
checks that can produce many reports in existing scripts.
.PP
Whole-script dataflow findings are strict. In the default mood, a script is
rejected when it reads a name that it never assigns, calls a function that reads
arguments without passing any, or calls a function before defining it. If an
unassigned name resembles an assigned name, that assignment is shown as a detail
location.
.PP
A function assignment without
.B local
is reported because it changes a global variable. Under a
.B sh
shebang or in the
.B sh
mood there is no
.BR local ,
so only a variable that is first set inside a function and also used outside it
is reported, with a suggestion to assign it before the function.
.PP
A comment of the form
.B # shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted-command-substitution
suppresses the named analysis checks. A numeric code suppresses every variant
under that code. A slug suppresses only its named variant. Native analysis
checks use their slugs. The optional
.B SC
prefix may be omitted. The value may be quoted, repeated, set to
.BR all ,
or contain a half-open range such as
.BR SC2000-SC2100 .
A directive before the first command applies to the complete file. A later
directive applies to the next complete and-or command. A directive after
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
or a pipe does not apply. These comments do not suppress parser or runtime
diagnostics outside the analysis catalog.
.PP
A diagnostic names the file, line, and column of the failing command, quotes
its source line, and puts a caret under it. Text inside command substitution,
backticks, process substitution, and here-document bodies is part of the file,
so its diagnostics use the file's own line and column. Text built at run time,
such as an eval string or a trap action, is its own source. A
.B \-c
string is named
.BR \-c ,
so a location in it reads
.BR \-c:1:5: .
Every
.B \-c
string uses that name, and the caret under the command line frame tells them
apart. Standard input and interactive lines have no name.
.PP
Every level of indirection between the top level and the failing command adds
one backtrace frame at the construct that entered it. The levels are a function
call, a sourced file, an eval, a trap action, a subshell, a command
substitution, a function substitution, and a process substitution. A trap
action also adds a frame at the
.B trap
command that installed it. A diagnostic with no indirection has no frame. A
script given on the command line adds no frame. The command line counts as a
level only when more than one script file or
.B \-c
string is given.
.PP
A frame begins with
.BR trace: .
Consecutive frames in one file print the file name once, in the first header of
the group, and the later headers show only the line and column. A repeated frame
is printed once and its header ends with the repeat count, as in
.BR "trace: repeated 4 times." .
A frame is printed once for each activation. Diagnostics print the same text
in every mood.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
The value supplies default command-line options.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANALYSIS
The variable carries the analysis settings changed with
.B set
to the shells it launches. Each use of
.B set
rewrites it from the current state as whitespace-separated words:
.B mimicry
for
.BR \-I ,
.BI warnings= N
for the level of
.BR \-W ,
.B no\-annoying
for
.BR "+o annoying\-diagnostics" ,
and
.B no\-diagnostics
for
.BR "\-o no\-diagnostics" .
The variable is removed when no word remains. A shell reads it at startup, and
an explicit
.B \-W
level replaces the inherited level, and the other options add to the inherited
words. A shell that starts with the variable
rewrites it from its own settings.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
The value names the command history file. The default is ~/.kosh_history.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE
The value bounds the number of history events retained for recall, listing, and
storage. The default is 4096. A value of zero disables history retention and
storage.
.TP
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
An interactive shell on a terminal marks each prompt, command, exit status, and
working directory with OSC 133 and OSC 7 sequences that terminals and IDE
terminals read. A value of 0 turns the marks off. They are never written when
standard output is not a terminal or TERM is dumb. See
.BR "Terminal shell integration" .
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR
These variables select the editor used by
.BR fc .
FCEDIT takes precedence over EDITOR.
.TP
.B KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The value names the z frecency store. The default is
~/.kosh_directory_history.
.TP
.B KOSH_CALC_HISTORY
The value names interactive calc history. The default is ~/.kosh_calc_history.
.TP
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ", " KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS
These variables configure the external tab selector. An interactive session sets
both before the first prompt when a startup file has not already set them.
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND names the program the candidates are handed to and
defaults to
.BR fzf .
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS holds the presentation options and defaults to
\-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=~40% \-\-min\-height=3. The value is
split on blanks with no expansion. The \-\-read0 and \-\-print0 record framing
is supplied separately and stays out of the value.
.TP
.BR ENV ", " BASH_ENV
These variables select startup files under the conditions in
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.TP
.B PATH
The value is a colon-separated command search path. The
.B \-\-no\-init\-files
option resets it to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " CDPATH
HOME supplies home paths. PWD and OLDPWD record the logical current and prior
directories. CDPATH supplies search directories for a relative
.B cd
operand. A relative directory is resolved against logical PWD. The form
.B cd ..
removes the last logical component. A logical PWD is accepted only when it is
absolute and identifies the physical current directory.
.TP
.BR SHELL ", " SHLVL
SHELL names the executable. The invocation path is used when SHELL was unset.
SHLVL is inherited and incremented by a child shell.
.TP
.B IFS
The value controls field splitting and the join performed by "$*". The default
contains space, tab, and newline.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
An interactive shell stores the initial terminal width and height in these
variables.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
The command runs before each interactive prompt. Its status does not replace the
previous command status.
.TP
.BR PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4
These variables provide the pre-execution, primary, continuation, select, and
xtrace prompts. PS0 defaults to empty. PS2 defaults to "> ". PS3 defaults to
"#? ". PS4 defaults to "+ ". The first byte of PS4 is repeated once per
enclosing subshell. The default PS1 shows the git branch, ahead and behind
counts, user, host, and working directory, and it ends with a bullet. The
backslash escapes \\u,
\\h, \\H, \\w, \\W, \\P, \\g, \\$, \\?, \\j, \\D, \\., \\t, \\T, \\@, \\A,
\\d, \\s, \\v, \\V, \\!, \\#, \\n, \\r, \\e, \\a, \\[, and \\] are expanded.
The \\. escape emits a bullet, plain when the last exit status is zero and
bold red when it is not.
.TP
.BR RPS1 ", " RPROMPT
RPS1 is the right prompt drawn at the right edge of the first input row.
RPROMPT is read when RPS1 is unset or empty. The value expands like PS1. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B PS1_TRANSIENT
The value is the prompt a submitted line is redrawn after when the
.B transient\-prompt
option is on. It expands like PS1. When it is unset or empty, the prompt is
"$ ", or "# " for root. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The value selects the xtrace descriptor. Standard error is used when it is unset
or invalid.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " NO_COLOR
TERM selects terminal capabilities on POSIX systems. A nonempty NO_COLOR
disables prompt, diagnostic, koshkit cat, and editor color. Reverse video
carries no color. The reversed row of the interactive selector stays visible.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Completion searches these manual paths for subcommands and options.
.TP
.B OPTERR
A value of zero silences the diagnostics of
.BR getopts .
A leading colon in the option string silences them too.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The value names the directory used for a temporary file. The default is /tmp.
.TP
.BR PIPESTATUS ", " REPLY ", " OPTIND ", " OPTARG ", " TIMEFORMAT
These variables hold pipeline statuses, default read and select input, getopts
state, and time formatting.
.TP
.BR KOSH ", " KOSH_VERSION ", " KOSH_COMMIT ", " KOSH_BUILD_MODE ", " KOSH_OS
These values identify the invocation path, version, build commit, build mode,
and build host distribution and kernel. The invocation path is absolute. A name
without a slash is found on PATH, and the running executable is used when the
search finds nothing.
.TP
.B KOSH_GIT_BRANCH
For a working directory inside a repository, the value contains the branch
name. A detached HEAD produces a short commit hash. The value is empty outside a
repository. An assignment overrides detection. The search for a repository
moves up from the working directory and never enters a directory listed in
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES. That variable holds absolute paths, separated the way
PATH is.
.TP
.BR KOSH_GIT_AHEAD ", " KOSH_GIT_BEHIND
The values contain the unpushed and unpulled commit counts relative to the
current branch's upstream. Both are empty outside a repository, when no upstream
is configured, and when the count is zero.
.TP
.B KOSH_IDENTITY
The read-only exported value is the lowercase CRC-32 identity of the running
executable. An inherited value is discarded.
.TP
.BR KOSH_WELCOME ", " KOSH_FAREWELL
When set and non-empty, the value is printed once at interactive startup
(KOSH_WELCOME) or exit (KOSH_FAREWELL), prefixed with the shell name. An empty
value prints nothing. When unset, the shell prints the built-in greeting for
the active mood on startup and a fixed farewell on exit. A non-zero exit code
is appended in parentheses to the farewell.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANSI_*
The names BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their BRIGHT_
forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET supply terminal escapes. A recognized name is empty
when color is disabled. An explicit assignment takes precedence.
.TP
.BR RANDOM ", " SRANDOM ", " SECONDS ", " EPOCHSECONDS ", " EPOCHREALTIME
These dynamic values supply random numbers, elapsed time, and wall clock time.
An assignment to
.B SECONDS
moves the base the elapsed count is measured from. An assignment to
.B RANDOM
seeds the generator, and a repeated seed repeats the sequence. Each subshell
and command substitution reseeds its own generator, so its numbers differ from
the ones the parent draws next. A
.B local
declaration of either name freezes it for the length of the call. An
.B unset
of
.B SECONDS
or
.B RANDOM
makes the name ordinary for the rest of the shell. The name then reads empty,
an assignment stores an ordinary value, and a subshell inherits it that way.
They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR IFS ", " LINENO
The field separator and the line number of the running command are available in
every mood.
.TP
.BR BASHPID ", " PPID ", " UID ", " EUID ", " GROUPS ", " HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
These dynamic values identify the current shell process, parent, ids,
groups, host, and platform. The value of $$ remains the original shell process
id. These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B GLOBIGNORE
The value lists colon\-separated patterns that pathname expansion removes from
its results. Assignment enables
.B dotglob
and unsetting disables it. The pathname expansion section describes the
matching.
.TP
.B HISTCMD
The value is the history number of the current command. A non-interactive shell
reports zero. An assignment is discarded. The name is unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR SHELLOPTS ", " BASHOPTS ", " BASH_SUBSHELL ", " BASH_ARGV0 ", " BASH_EXECUTION_STRING ", " BASH_MONOSECONDS
These values expose set options, shopt options, subshell depth, argument zero,
the current command string, and a monotonic millisecond count. The two option
lists are read-only. These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR COPROC ", " COPROC_PID
The
.B coproc
command binds these to the descriptors and the process id of an unnamed
coprocess. A named coprocess binds NAME and NAME_PID and leaves these unset.
These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_ALIASES
This associative array contains the current aliases. Assigning an element
defines or replaces that alias. Unsetting an element has no effect. Unsetting
the whole variable disconnects the array without deleting the aliases. A later
.B declare \-A BASH_ALIASES
creates an ordinary array that is not connected to aliases. The name is
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR BASH_ARGC ", " BASH_ARGV
.B BASH_ARGC
contains the positional parameter count for each recorded call frame.
.B BASH_ARGV
contains the recorded arguments in reverse order, with the innermost frame
first. The root shell is the last frame. Changing positional parameters does
not change arguments already recorded for a frame.
.PP
The
.B extdebug
shopt option records function calls and sourced-file calls. A source call with
operands records those operands. A source call without operands records its
path as one argument. Without
.BR extdebug ,
function calls are not recorded. A source call without operands records its
path while the file runs. The first root source call with operands establishes
those operands as the base frame. Later source calls with operands are not
recorded. Enabling
.B extdebug
before the arrays have been initialized starts with the current frame.
.PP
Assignments and declarations with values have no effect. Unset and local
declarations fail. The names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B DIRSTACK
This indexed array contains the current directory at index zero followed by the
saved directories that
.B dirs
prints. Assigning index zero or an index past the end has no effect. Assigning
another existing index changes that saved stack entry. Whole-array assignment
changes only the existing indices, and append cannot grow the stack. Unsetting
an element has no effect. A local declaration shadows the live array until the
function returns. Unsetting the whole variable disconnects the array without
clearing the directory stack. A later
.B declare \-a DIRSTACK
creates an ordinary array that is not connected to the stack. The name is
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR FUNCNAME ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_COMMAND
These values describe the active call stack and command. Their array forms
contain the complete stack. The text of BASH_COMMAND is the source as it was
written. A compound command is laid out by Bash over several indented lines,
and that layout is not reproduced. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The array contains groups from the last [[ ... =~ ... ]] match.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
These values report a Bash identity. They are initialized when the runtime mood
or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR COMP_WORDS ", " COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDBREAKS ", " COMPREPLY
A programmable completion function reads these values and writes COMPREPLY.
COMP_WORDS contains the words on the line. COMP_CWORD is the index of the word
under the cursor. COMP_LINE contains the complete line. COMP_POINT is the
cursor byte offset. COMP_WORDBREAKS contains the bytes that split a word.
Trailing spaces are removed from each COMPREPLY entry, and the space after an
accepted candidate is controlled by the space-after-completion option. When
.B compopt -o filenames
is run inside the function, a slash is appended to each entry that names a
directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran. A command path that
names a directory, or has a trailing separator whose target is not a directory,
returns 126. A script file that cannot be opened returns 127. A usage error in a
non-rescue invocation returns 2. A child terminated by SIGPIPE returns 141. A
.B \-\-lint
batch returns 1 when an input cannot be read or parsed, or its analysis reports
an error. Warning-only findings return 0.
The
.B \-\-format
mode returns 1 when an input cannot be read, parsed, formatted, or replaced.
The
.B \-\-apply
mode also returns 1 when a safe replacement cannot be completed. These modes
return 0 after every requested input succeeds. An invalid option combination
returns 2.
.SH ERRORS
An unavailable command or pathname is reported through its first unavailable
component when that component can be identified. A missing explicit directory
may include one close sibling suggestion. A source caret covers the relevant
component or argument when source mapping is available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_history
This is the default command history file.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_directory_history
This is the default z frecency store.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_calc_history
This is the default interactive calc history file.
.PP
Startup configuration files are listed in
.BR kosh (5).
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B kosh
and
.B bash
moods accept Bash syntax in addition to POSIX syntax. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled. The
.B sh
mood uses the supported dash-compatible behavior.
.PP
The 0b arithmetic prefix is an implementation extension. Bash writes the same
value with a 2# prefix. The
.BR enable ,
.BR bind ,
and selected
.B help
flags are accepted compatibility forms with the effects described under
.BR BUILTINS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (5),
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
